---
[protocol] Speak MCP over stdio (complete)
Implement the JSON-RPC and MCP lifecycle boundary.
Serves: an MCP client can initialize the server and discover its tools.
Absent: the library has no process protocol an agent can call.
Known by: initialize, notifications/initialized, ping, tools/list, and
 tools/call are newline-delimited JSON-RPC messages with no stdout logging.
> The supported MCP protocol version is 2025-06-18.
> Protocol failures are JSON-RPC errors; tool execution failures are successful
> tools/call responses with isError true, so an agent can inspect a refusal.
> The server advertises exactly one tool for each public library verb.
---
[handle-wire] Carry handles across messages (complete)
Give a returned handle a stable MCP representation and reconstruct it at the
next tool call.
Serves: provenance and neighbours can consume a handle returned by search.
Absent: the MCP process would be able to return a handle but not use it.
Known by: a handle survives JSON serialization and rehydration, while a
reversed span or malformed digest is rejected before a library verb runs.
-> protocol
> The wire object is origin, start, end, and lower-case hexadecimal digest.
> The core library owns Handle::from_parts. It validates only the structural
> span order; the library verbs retain the decision between Stale and Gone.
---
[adapter] Compose the public library into tools (complete)
Build the scry-mcp binary and its five tools over the public scry API.
Serves: an MCP client can add, delete, search, widen, and trace corpus content.
Absent: the protocol would terminate at metadata rather than reach the corpus.
Known by: the process requires explicit store and model-cache paths, loads the
pinned embed seam once, opens the store, and keeps all store internals private.
-> handle-wire
-> protocol
> The binary is a separate workspace member, so this boundary cannot use
> crates/scry private operations. It uses serde and serde_json from centralized
> workspace dependencies.
> Tool arguments are strict objects. ttl_seconds is explicit, count is positive,
> and successful results use structuredContent with a text content item beside it.
> Startup incompatibility ends before an MCP session, matching the library's
> terminal open result rather than inventing a tool refusal.
---
[e2e] Drive the real process (complete)
Validate the complete library flow through the actual MCP binary.
Serves: the MCP adapter is more than a parser and the library remains usable
through a process boundary.
Absent: protocol tests could pass while add, handle exchange, persistence, or
refusals were disconnected.
Known by: one spawned-process test performs initialize, tool discovery, add,
search, provenance, neighbours, delete, and the resulting Gone response over a
real redb file and real model seam.
-> adapter
> The test takes the handle from search structuredContent and sends it back as
> the provenance and neighbours argument, so handle serialization is measured.
> The test uses the model cache filled by the library suite when present and
> downloads the pinned files on a clean machine; it claims no hermetic run.
> Measured on 2026-08-21: the MCP test passes, the workspace has 123 library
> unit tests, 10 library integration tests, and 1 MCP integration test, and
> formatting, clippy, docs, and cargo-deny all pass.
---
[set-mcp-contract] Fix the batch add MCP contract (complete)
Propagate the accepted set-upsert wire contract into the MCP product document.
Files: docs/product-planning/mcp.md.
Do: define the exact origins plus ttl_seconds input, preflight origin validation and normalization, canonical item order, complete versus halted reports, per-item refusal and io errors, structuredContent retention, isError behavior, and non-cancellation on disconnect.
Accept: the document agrees with the library contract and leaves no unresolved wire or tool-result semantic choice for the adapter.
-> e2e
> Implemented and validated docs/product-planning/mcp.md: the add schema is exactly origins plus ttl_seconds, with preflight normalization, complete/halted reports, per-item errors, and non-cancellation semantics. The document passed git diff --check.
---
[set-mcp-adapter] Adapt batch reports to MCP (complete)
Adapt the library set report to MCP through the minimal supported Store facade.
Prerequisite: library.vine set-checkpoint must be complete before this node starts implementation, including the private one-abstraction-per-verb decision.
Files: crates/scry-mcp/src/server.rs and crates/scry-mcp/src/wire.rs, with focused adapter tests.
Do: parse the strict argument object, validate every raw origin before invoking Store::add, fold normalized duplicates into a BTreeSet, serialize every AddOutcome, derive complete or halted status, preserve partial reports in structuredContent, and keep protocol errors distinct from tool results. Isolate wire policy in private helpers rather than adding public backend traits.
Accept: the advertised schema is exact, no invalid request can start a member, refusals are non-error reports, halted reports set isError true without losing prior items, and no async or cancellation machinery is introduced.
-> adapter
-> set-mcp-contract
> Resolved the earlier adapter test-shape gap without widening the public library surface: private wire fixtures exercise complete and halted report serialization, while the library tests exercise actual Failed and Uncertain causes. The adapter calls the supported Store facade and keeps protocol.rs unchanged.
